[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-22703"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-2{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2{e}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/c199b110a3d19ecc23454f52dc7470af318ce871805595fd5fe7609dee5b0545.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${e}^{x}<1$ when $x<0.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${e}^{x}>1,$ which has the solution $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-22809"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0$ when $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ which has the solution $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-114061/selection/382d45f49cce0f6814739c2c7fde7bf84c4bdcd3b8a5cd0deda738d8ba233bd2" :answer/id #uuid "e284197a-b514-5aea-a5f7-f0a53fe3e40c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114061/selection/0e3d399c6883a273e58f62dca7460fdc20d9313c49abda9116dd30c97ceba130" :answer/id #uuid "2253a318-f02f-50db-96d3-30c172a6e1bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114061/selection/777e8eb0b47756d164247cb94188ad40db7b4909b9f10dc4e76d26f8f7d72712" :answer/id #uuid "7f496681-b587-5a13-a6f3-2a4102065d79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114061/selection/c6b011814c1ef6655864882f2620ae9eccc8caecf7241756483444daf0a4908a" :answer/id #uuid "c9d3e377-1a61-5e5c-98de-267800eef5f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114061/selection/65e4e842cf6a8ff550b8c78aff207229ef9a060f4624aa3b4808953ceaf20a09" :answer/id #uuid "81dc2f5e-0e87-51e0-9055-b0ae905a2c98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-2,2),$ and $(4,\\infty )$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114061/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c4c27b-802b-576d-a604-f7f8dd87a004" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114061/selection/382d45f49cce0f6814739c2c7fde7bf84c4bdcd3b8a5cd0deda738d8ba233bd2" :answer-field/choices ["q-114061/selection/382d45f49cce0f6814739c2c7fde7bf84c4bdcd3b8a5cd0deda738d8ba233bd2" "q-114061/selection/0e3d399c6883a273e58f62dca7460fdc20d9313c49abda9116dd30c97ceba130" "q-114061/selection/777e8eb0b47756d164247cb94188ad40db7b4909b9f10dc4e76d26f8f7d72712" "q-114061/selection/c6b011814c1ef6655864882f2620ae9eccc8caecf7241756483444daf0a4908a" "q-114061/selection/65e4e842cf6a8ff550b8c78aff207229ef9a060f4624aa3b4808953ceaf20a09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114061" :question/id #uuid "8d160f05-f6c3-5cab-b428-a1db0a9e077b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114061" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/d3bd98f5662140655dd2bd33f99dc8704b1bd03b6004133dc8492ac51ea3a347.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={h}^{″}(x).$  Find the intervals on which the function $h(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $h(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${h}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={h}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${h}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4).$  Therefore, the function $h(x)$ is concave down on the intervals $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-114061/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114061"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114259/selection/6b037f504fd0046ec2df74dd6e76238ec09750c1612acc4bb3cf47721d6b7a33" :answer/id #uuid "8c64dcb9-5002-5251-8bd5-055a5e23e4ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114259/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "d1969ae4-e225-54f6-ad32-1e7d2504ae08" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114259/selection/301772469cdcfaf57f8f58be63997eb4eba52678eeb14a2b2c2e3c41772aa6f3" :answer/id #uuid "e2c7b6b8-1095-5772-b8aa-6b571b8ba6d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114259/selection/e8b6e48d2640b190477b067d73ed38c8ae23d6a2118701d65bc1f8aac439d661" :answer/id #uuid "b79973a8-c61d-525f-93c4-21e97b5b7ab6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114259/selection/7d721447c74511cc7ce1dd365eb9260ed7acbcf4fe9ece91f3ee8c2e43a7d309" :answer/id #uuid "140b671d-75df-535b-819b-eeeb1e4a6998" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114259/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "58c9bf5e-c365-5940-9048-56324041d229" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114259/selection/7d721447c74511cc7ce1dd365eb9260ed7acbcf4fe9ece91f3ee8c2e43a7d309" :answer-field/choices ["q-114259/selection/6b037f504fd0046ec2df74dd6e76238ec09750c1612acc4bb3cf47721d6b7a33" "q-114259/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-114259/selection/301772469cdcfaf57f8f58be63997eb4eba52678eeb14a2b2c2e3c41772aa6f3" "q-114259/selection/e8b6e48d2640b190477b067d73ed38c8ae23d6a2118701d65bc1f8aac439d661" "q-114259/selection/7d721447c74511cc7ce1dd365eb9260ed7acbcf4fe9ece91f3ee8c2e43a7d309"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114259" :question/id #uuid "c6fc0242-cf86-5ce2-ba38-34bc05e4e489" :question/math-academy-id "q-114259" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/a7330541d6102141aec87f6733d09585553de672c5e55d2f84fe70196e4c6b5f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[-2,2].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(-1,1).$ Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval  $(-1,1)$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114259/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114259"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "50c46685-68d7-5711-ad91-969cc9ecc2fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee393f89-5082-5275-b0e7-31835628e523" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344783/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-344783/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "2cf4ebdb-0183-5680-98b8-3b8fe01f1e70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb4591cf-15d8-5adf-81ca-b6fca3593610" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344783/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-344783/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a1bd6f2f-67a5-5bfb-ab67-9a1279a72e60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5010bd79-fdf6-59a1-8373-aeccc9f0398a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344783/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344783/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783/field-4/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "c71325f8-8309-58d1-970b-bf091d198c1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8a7367-fa6d-565c-ab1d-b1e100a6bb24" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344783/field-4/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-344783/field-4/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344783" :question/id #uuid "47567230-6e1c-5ccd-bbfb-9bdb7523ea72" :question/math-academy-id "q-344783" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/345b36f79184e0857bfc63693b2be0a968ba308035af9548136c5b7ba692ab52.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-5,$ $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $x=4,$ and $x=6,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,-5),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,6).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(4,6).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals \n\n$$\n(\\,-\\infty ,-5\\,),\\,(\\,-1,1\\,),\\,(\\,4,6\\,).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344783/field-1" "q-344783/field-2" "q-344783/field-3" "q-344783/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344783"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
